"book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","best","best","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","good","best","best","best","inaccuracy","best","good","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","good","good","best","best","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","brilliant","best","great","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","blunder","best","excellent","best","good","brilliant","best","good"],"moments":[{"ply":67,"name":"34.exf6","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"34.exf6!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the rook on a1 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 34…Qxa1 35.g5 Rd7 36.gxh6 the engine rates it +1.36.","Takes the pawn on f6.","Pushes a pawn towards the Black king, to pry open lines in front of it.","34.exf6 pushes the pawn storm towards the king and leaves Black with an isolated pawn on e6.","Leaves Black with an isolated pawn on e6: a long-term weakness."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"34.exf6!! ignores the attack on the rook on a1 and takes the f6 pawn first. Black can take only one of the two, and either way White is a little better."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"The rook on a1 is attacked by the queen on b2, and 34.exf6 doesn't save it. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the f6 pawn first. Now Black can take only one of the two: the pawn on f6 or the rook on a1."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"34…Qxa1 is met by 35.g5, threatening gxh6, winning the queen for a bishop. Then 36.Qg4 threatens Qxe6+, winning a rook, and 37.gxh6 takes the h6 pawn. Material is level and White is clearly better (+2.79). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"If Black takes back on f6","text":"34…gxf6 is met by 35.Re1, and the rook on a1 gets away. Then 37.Re3 threatens Rxc3, winning the queen, and 38.Rxd3 takes the rook on d3. White is still two pawns down, but the attack is worth more: White is clearly better (+2.28)."}]},{"ply":69,"name":"35.g5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"35.g5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Protects the f6 pawn, which was hanging.","A pawn break: it challenges the h6 pawn to open lines.","The next best move, 35.Qc7, would drop White's winning chances from 66% to 43% (−0.76).","35.g5 wins a rook for a pawn, picking it up with 36.gxh6, and threatens gxh6, winning a pawn.","Sets up a sequence and wins a rook for a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 36.gxh6.","It threatens gxh6, winning a pawn, so Black has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":78,"name":"39…Ne5","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"39…Ne5 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 37% to 10%.","points":["White answers 40.h6, which pushes the passed pawn to h6, close to promotion.","After h6, White wins 3 points of material within 6 moves.","Better was 39…c3 (+1.44), which pushes the passed pawn to c3, close to promotion."],"better":"39…c3 pushes the passed pawn to c3. Engine line: 40.Bd3 e5 41.f4 Nd8 42.Kf2 Kh8 (+1.44)."},{"ply":83,"name":"42.Nxe6+","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"42.Nxe6+!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the knight on e6 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: Black's best reply is 42…Ke7 43.Nc7 Nf3+ 44.Kg2 instead (the engine rates it +8.80).","Takes the pawn on e6.","Creates a passed pawn on e4: no enemy pawn can stop it any more.","42.Nxe6+ wins a pawn.","Takes the e6 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"42.Nxe6+!! offers the knight on e6. Taking it lets White's pawn become a queen."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"42.Nxe6+ puts the knight on e6 where it can be taken by the queen on f6. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"42…Qxe6 is met by 43.h8=Q+. Then 44.Qgg7+ gives check, and 45.Qgf8+ gives check. White ends up 6 points of material ahead: White is winning (+11.38)."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the knight alone and play 42…Ke7. White answers 43.Nc7, and the knight on e6 gets away, threatening Nd5+, winning the queen. Then 47.Bxh3 takes the queen on h3. White ends up a pawn ahead: White is winning (+8.80)."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 42.Bh3, is clearly weaker (+7.17 against +8.80). Black answers 42…Ke7, preparing …Nac6 and …Nb4. White would still be winning, but far less than after 42.Nxe6+."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Sicilian Defense: Delayed Alapin Variation, with e6","eco":"B40","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7…dxc3: 14 moves in all, 7 by White and 7 by Black.","Theory runs out after 7…dxc3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}